## Introduction

This guide walks through how I used `git`, GitHub, and the terminal to manage a project. For each step I'll explain what I did and why I did it that way, so it's clear I actually understand what's going on rather than just copying commands.

---

## Step 1: Creating the RStudio Project and `example.qmd`

I started by creating a new RStudio Project — going to **File > New Project > New Directory > New Project** and naming it `assignment2`. The reason I used an RStudio Project rather than just a plain folder is that it keeps everything self-contained. All file paths are relative to the project root, so if someone else opens it on their computer, nothing breaks.

Inside the project I created this file, `example.qmd`. Quarto files let you mix normal text with R code, and then render it all into an HTML page. I hit the **Render** button in RStudio to make sure it actually worked before doing anything with git.

Here's a bit of R code just to show the file renders properly:

```{r}
summary(cars)
```

Once I could see the HTML output in the browser, I knew the setup was good and I could move on.

---

## Step 2: Setting Up Git and Pushing to GitHub

With a working project, the next thing I wanted to do was put it under version control. The whole point of git is that it keeps a record of every change I make, so I can always go back if I mess something up. And pushing to GitHub means there's a copy online — useful both as a backup and for sharing with others.

I opened the terminal and ran:

```bash
# Turn this folder into a git repository
git init
```

This creates a hidden `.git` folder where git stores all the history. Nothing is tracked yet at this point — `git init` just sets up the structure.

```bash
# Stage everything in the folder
git add .
```

`git add .` tells git which files to include in the next commit. Think of it like picking what goes into a box before you seal it. The `.` means "everything in the current folder".

```bash
# Save the first snapshot
git commit -m "Initial commit: add project files and example.qmd"
```

`git commit` takes a snapshot of everything I staged. The message after `-m` describes what's in this commit. Writing a clear message is important — when I look back at the history later, I want to understand what each commit was actually for without having to dig through the files.

```bash
# Connect the local repo to GitHub
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/assignment2.git

# Push it up
git push -u origin main
```

`git remote add origin` tells git where GitHub is. Then `git push -u origin main` sends the commit up to GitHub. The `-u` flag sets up a connection between my local `main` branch and the one on GitHub, so next time I can just type `git push` without specifying everything.

---

## Step 3: Creating `testbranch`, Making Changes, and Pushing

At this point I wanted to start making changes to `example.qmd`, but I didn't want to mess with the version on `main` while I was experimenting. That's exactly what branches are for — I can work on a separate branch, and `main` stays untouched until I'm ready to bring the changes in.

```bash
# Create a new branch called testbranch and switch to it
git switch -c testbranch
```

`git switch -c testbranch` creates the branch and moves me onto it in one go. Any commits I make from here will go onto `testbranch`, not `main`.

I then edited `example.qmd` and added a new section about the cars dataset. After saving the file:

```bash
# Check what's changed before staging
git status

# Stage the file
git add example.qmd

# Commit the change
git commit -m "Add dataset introduction section on testbranch"

# Push testbranch to GitHub
git push -u origin testbranch
```

I always run `git status` before staging, just to double-check I'm only adding what I meant to. The `-u origin testbranch` on the push makes sure the branch shows up on GitHub too, not just on my machine.

---

## Step 4: Adding the Data Folder and Amending the Commit

After I committed, I realised I'd forgotten to include the `data` folder with my Assignment 1 data. I could have just made a new commit, but that felt messy — it would just be a commit that says "oops, forgot this". Instead I used `git commit --amend` to add the data folder to the commit I just made, like I'd included it from the start.

```bash
# Create the data folder and copy the data in
mkdir data
cp /path/to/your/assignment1data.csv data/

# Stage the data folder
git add data/

# Add it to the previous commit without changing the message
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

`--amend` rewrites the last commit to include the new staged files. `--no-edit` means I keep the same commit message. The catch is that rewriting a commit changes its hash, so the version on GitHub is now different from my local one. A normal push would get rejected.

```bash
# Force push to overwrite the old commit on GitHub
git push --force origin testbranch
```

`git push --force` overwrites what's on GitHub with my updated version. I only felt comfortable doing this because I'm the only one working on `testbranch` — if someone else was also working on it, force pushing could wipe out their commits, which would be a problem.

