## Step 5: Switching Back to `main` and Making a Conflicting Change

Now I switched back to `main` and changed `example.qmd` in a way that would conflict with what I did on `testbranch`. The whole point of this step is to set up a merge conflict on purpose, so I can practise dealing with one.

```bash
# Switch back to main
git switch main
```

I edited the same part of `example.qmd` that I'd changed on `testbranch`, but wrote something different. For example, `testbranch` had "This dataset contains car speed data", and on `main` I wrote "This section explores the cars dataset". Same lines, different content — that's what causes a conflict.

```bash
git status
git add example.qmd
git commit -m "Modify example.qmd on main: update dataset description"
git push origin main
```

Now both branches have different versions of the same lines. When I try to merge them, git won't know which one to keep.

---

## Step 6: Merging `testbranch` into `main` and Fixing the Conflict

```bash
git switch main
git merge testbranch
```

Git immediately hit a conflict and stopped:

```
Auto-merging example.qmd
CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in example.qmd
Automatic merge failed; fix conflicts then commit the result.
```

When I opened `example.qmd`, I could see git had marked the problem area like this:

```
<<<<<<< HEAD
This section explores the cars dataset
=======
This dataset contains car speed data
>>>>>>> testbranch
```

The bit between `<<<<<<< HEAD` and `=======` is what's on `main`. The bit between `=======` and `>>>>>>> testbranch` is what came from `testbranch`. Git can't decide which one is right, so it leaves it for me to sort out.

I fixed it by combining both into one sentence that made sense:

```
This section explores the cars dataset, which contains car speed data.
```

I deleted the conflict markers, saved the file, then finished the merge:

```bash
git add example.qmd
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict: combine dataset descriptions from main and testbranch"
git push origin main
```

After pushing, I checked GitHub and could see both branches were now merged with the full history intact.

---

## Step 7: Tagging the Commit as `v1.0`

Now that the merge was done and the project was in a good state, I wanted to mark this point as version 1.0. Tags are a way of pinning a label to a specific commit — handy for marking milestones or releases so I can find them easily later.

```bash
# Create an annotated tag
git tag -a v1.0 -m "Version 1.0: project complete, merge conflict resolved"

# Push the tag to GitHub
git push origin v1.0
```

I used an annotated tag (`-a`) rather than a lightweight one because annotated tags store extra info like the date, who made the tag, and a message. Tags don't get pushed with a normal `git push`, so I had to push it separately.

---

## Step 8: Deleting `testbranch` Locally and on the Remote

`testbranch` had been merged into `main` and I didn't need it anymore. Leaving old branches around just makes things harder to navigate, especially if the project grows, so I deleted it from both places.

```bash
# Delete it locally
git branch -d testbranch

# Delete it from GitHub
git push origin --delete testbranch
```

I used lowercase `-d` instead of `-D` because the lowercase version checks that the branch has been merged before deleting. If I accidentally tried to delete a branch that hadn't been merged yet, git would warn me rather than just deleting it. That's a good safety net.

---

## Step 9: Showing the Commit Log in Condensed Form

To see a summary of everything that happened, I ran:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

The output looked something like:

```
a3f1c2d (HEAD -> main, tag: v1.0, origin/main) Resolve merge conflict: combine dataset descriptions
8b2e4a1 Modify example.qmd on main: update dataset description
5d9c7f3 Add dataset introduction section on testbranch
1a4b8e2 Initial commit: add project files and example.qmd
```

`--oneline` puts each commit on a single line with its short hash and message. It's the quickest way to get a picture of the whole project history without scrolling through loads of detail.

