## Step 10: Adding a Plot, Committing, and Undoing the Commit

I added a new section to `example.qmd` with a simple plot:

```{r}
plot(pressure,
     main = "Vapour Pressure of Mercury vs Temperature",
     xlab = "Temperature (°C)",
     ylab = "Pressure (mm Hg)",
     col = "steelblue",
     pch = 16)
```

Then I committed it:

```bash
git add example.qmd
git commit -m "Add pressure plot section to example.qmd"
```

Then I practised undoing that commit without losing the changes I'd made to the file:

```bash
git reset HEAD~1
```

`git reset HEAD~1` moves the branch pointer back one commit, so the commit disappears from the history. But because I didn't add `--hard`, it keeps the file changes sitting in my working directory — the plot code is still in `example.qmd`, it's just uncommitted now. This is useful if I realise I want to change something before it goes into the history permanently.

I checked it worked:

```bash
git status
git log --oneline
```

`git status` showed `example.qmd` as modified but unstaged, and `git log --oneline` confirmed the plot commit was gone. The file itself was fine — nothing was lost.

