## Summary

Here's a quick rundown of what I covered and why each thing matters:

- **`git init` + `git remote add`** — sets up the repo and connects it to GitHub
- **`git add` + `git commit -m`** — stages and saves changes with a clear message
- **`git status`** — checks what's changed before staging, good habit to avoid mistakes
- **`git switch -c`** — creates a branch and switches to it, keeps experiments away from `main`
- **`git push -u origin`** — pushes a branch and sets up the tracking link
- **`git commit --amend`** — fixes the last commit when you forget something
- **`git push --force`** — needed after amending, only safe on branches you own
- **`git merge` + conflict resolution** — combines branches and handles conflicts manually
- **`git tag -a`** — marks a milestone with a label that stays in the history
- **`git branch -d` + `git push origin --delete`** — cleans up merged branches safely
- **`git log --oneline`** — shows a compact history of all commits
- **`git reset HEAD~1`** — undoes a commit while keeping the file changes
